text:
maria: Customer on Vercel Edge reports streaming POST to /v1/streams yields repeated 401s and the ReadableStream closes after ~2s. Works on node server runtimes. They're using @redwood/inference-js 1.4.0 in an Edge Function. Any known regressions?

devon: Can you paste the client/edge code and the headers they're sending? Also which auth method — API key or ephemeral token?

maria: API key in env (REDWOOD_API_KEY). They said regular non-streaming endpoints are fine from the same edge function. Our gateway logs show '401: invalid signature' for the streaming calls. Pasting their minimal repro below.

maria: ```js
export default async function handler(req) {
  const res = await fetch("https://api.redwood.ai/v1/streams", {
    method: "POST",
    headers: {
      "Authorization": `Bearer ${process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY}`,
      "Content-Type": "application/json",
    },
    body: JSON.stringify({ model: "gpt-4o-mini", prompt: "hi" })
  });

  const reader = res.body.getReader();
  while (true) {
    const { done, value } = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;
    console.log(new TextDecoder().decode(value));
  }
}
```

devon: Two quick suspicions: 1) Vercel Edge runtimes can behave differently for env access and header forwarding (but if non-stream works that's less likely). 2) More likely: bundlers/polyfills swap out the global fetch or web-crypto used for signing, which can change the signature bytes. Any evidence of node-fetch or polyfill in their bundle?

olivia: Also note there was a prior bug where our TS SDK (streaming path) used URLSearchParams / object property iteration that could reorder params under certain bundlers and break signing. Which exact package+build hash are they on?

maria: They're on @redwood/inference-js@1.4.0. I asked them to run a curl with the same Authorization header — curl -> 200, so key itself is valid. But our edge gateway shows the incoming Authorization header value truncated in some requests (e.g. 'Bear' or missing tail) which matches the 401.

devon: If curl works but edge requests appear truncated on our gateway, could be CDN/proxy clipping long headers on proxied streaming routes or the Edge runtime mutating the header when doing streaming fetch. Ask them to log raw request headers right before calling fetch. Also check for any middleware that strips/rewrites Authorization.

maria: They logged raw headers; in the edge function it shows full header: Authorization: 'Bearer abcdefghijklmnop'. Our gateway received different value. They also noticed their request sets no explicit Connection header.

edge-bot: deploy:edge - rolling update active in 3 regions (08:17 UTC).

devon: Two actionable things: 1) Short-term workaround: move the call to a server runtime (not Edge) or pre-sign on server and hand a signed URL to the Edge function. 2) Recommended fix: upgrade to @redwood/inference-js@1.4.2 (contains edge-bundler signing fix) OR import the explicit edge bundle if they bundle: `import { Redwood } from '@redwood/inference-js/edge'`. Also ask them to ensure their bundler isn't injecting node polyfills for fetch/webcrypto.

maria: I'll send upgrade instructions + this repro checklist to the customer (log raw headers, try server runtime, upgrade to 1.4.2, try edge import). If they can't upgrade immediately, we'll route them to the server proxy workaround.

olivia: :thumbsup: please add a quick note to the 1.4.2 release notes about the bundler signing edge case so solutions can reference it.

devon: If they can share a small zipped bundle or a build trace, I can look for polyfill imports. Thanks.

maria: Will follow up with the customer and re-open if it persists.
